{
  "dataset": "24-bin equal-width wavelength binning proposal for spectral ray tracing, 380-780 nm",
  "ev": "derived",
  "calc": "380-780 nm split into 24 EQUAL-width bins (16.6667 nm each), because equal width is the simplest scheme to explain on the page and to index from a wavelength in a shader (bin = floor((lambda-380)/width)), and it keeps every visible sub-range represented even though the eye's sensitivity is very uneven across it (the brief's own WACR 16-bin-minimum spec is a bin COUNT, not a weighting scheme). Center = arithmetic midpoint of the bin, for the same reason (a Y-weighted centroid would pull centers toward 555 nm and complicate the index math for a small accuracy gain). cmf_integral is the EXACT bin-integrated (x_bar,y_bar,z_bar) -- computed by linearly interpolating the 1 nm CIE 1931 table to 0.1 nm and Riemann-summing -- so a renderer that assigns one spectral radiance value L_i to bin i can reconstruct XYZ = sum_i L_i * cmf_integral_i exactly enough for display, without re-integrating CMFs per frame. luminance_share is cmf_integral.Y normalized to sum to 1 across all bins, i.e. how much each bin actually contributes to perceived brightness under a flat (equal-radiance) spectrum -- useful for deciding where noise will be most visible if bins are stochastically sampled rather than all traced every frame.",
